.TH T-PGSQL 1 "November 2026" "t-pgsql 3.15.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
t-pgsql \- PostgreSQL database sync, backup and clone tool
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B t-pgsql
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B t-pgsql
.B \-\-batch
.IR job | all
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B t-pgsql
dumps, restores, clones, streams and masks PostgreSQL databases, locally or over
SSH, and runs batches of saved jobs from a YAML file.
It also sends notifications (Telegram, Slack, webhook, e-mail) and can run a
Telegram bot that triggers backups from a chat.
.PP
A local or TCP connection is written as
.PP
.RS
.B [user@]host[:port]/database
.RE
.PP
A database reached through SSH is written as
.PP
.RS
.B ssh://[ssh_user@]ssh_host[:ssh_port]/[db_user@]db_host[:db_port]/database
.RE
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B dump
Create a database backup (pg_dump).
.TP
.B restore
Restore a backup to a database (pg_restore).
.TP
.B clone
Dump and restore in one operation (full sync).
.TP
.B upgrade
Logical major-version migration: a clone that also migrates cluster globals
(roles, tablespaces) and refuses to restore into an older major version.
This is the dump/restore path, not a replacement for pg_upgrade or logical replication.
.TP
.B fetch
Fetch an existing dump file from a remote server without creating a new dump.
.TP
.BR batch " [" \fIjob | all ]
Run one saved job, or all of them, from the jobs file.
.TP
.B bot
Run the Telegram bot, which listens for chat commands and inline button callbacks
and starts backup jobs. It runs until it is stopped.
.TP
.B jobs
Manage saved jobs:
.B jobs list
(the default),
.BI "jobs show " name
and
.BI "jobs remove " name .
.TP
.B list
List dump files in the output directory.
.TP
.B meta
Show the metadata of a dump (the newest one, or the one given with
.BR \-\-file ).
.TP
.BR clean " [\fIdatabase\fR]"
Remove old dumps. A target is required (a database name,
.B \-\-from
or
.BR \-\-dump\-name )
together with
.B \-\-keep
or
.BR \-\-retention .
.TP
.B doctor
Run read-only checks of the environment and backup health: required and optional
tools, output directory and free disk space, freshness of the newest dump of each
database, readability of the newest archives and the jobs file.
With
.B \-\-from
and
.B \-\-to
it also tests connectivity.
.TP
.BR status " [\fIoptions\fR]"
Show the last run of each job from the run history (read-only; it creates nothing).
Columns: name, last run, result (ok, failed, suspect, skipped, locked, never),
duration, size, dumps kept and a note.
See
.BR "STATUS OPTIONS" .
.TP
.BR explain " [\fItopic\fR]"
Print a built-in explanation of what a command is for. Without a topic the topics
are listed (dump, restore, clone, stream, upgrade, fetch, batch, bot, list, meta,
clean, doctor, status, ping, lock, masking, retention).
.TP
.B version
Show the version.
.TP
.B help
Show the help message.
.SH CONNECTION OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-from " connection"
Source connection.
.TP
.BI \-\-to " connection"
Target connection. May be given several times to restore to several targets.
.SH PASSWORD OPTIONS
Passwords given on the command line are visible to other users in the process list.
Prefer password files or the environment variables listed under
.BR ENVIRONMENT .
When several sources are present, the order is: command-line password, environment
variable, password file, interactive prompt.
.TP
.BI \-\-password " password"
Password for both connections.
.TP
.BI \-\-from\-password " password"
Source password.
.TP
.BI \-\-to\-password " password"
Target password.
.TP
.BI \-\-password\-file " file"
Read the password from a file.
.TP
.BI \-\-from\-password\-file " file"
Read the source password from a file.
.TP
.BI \-\-to\-password\-file " file"
Read the target password from a file. May be given once for all targets or once
per
.BR \-\-to .
.TP
.BI \-\-config " file"
Per-run defaults file (simple key: value lines). It is not the batch jobs file; use
.B \-\-yaml
for that. Command-line options override it.
.SH FILTERING OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude\-table " tables"
Exclude tables (comma-separated).
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude\-schema " schemas"
Exclude schemas (comma-separated).
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude\-data " tables"
Exclude table data but keep the structure. Supports the schema.* wildcard.
.TP
.BI \-\-only\-table " tables"
Include only these tables.
.TP
.BI \-\-only\-schema " schemas"
Include only these schemas.
.SH COMPRESSION OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-compress " type"
Compression type: gzip (default), zstd, xz, bzip2 or none.
.TP
.BI \-\-compress\-level " n"
Level for the selected compressor (default 6). gzip, xz and bzip2: 1-9; zstd: 1-19.
.TP
.BI \-\-pg\-compress\-level " n"
Override pg_dump's built-in compression level, 0-9 (gzip only; default 6).
.TP
.BI \-\-compress\-where " where"
For SSH dumps with zstd, xz or bzip2: "source" compresses on the source host before
the copy, "target" (the default) compresses locally after it. Falls back to target
if the tool is missing on the source.
.SH STORAGE OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-output " directory"
Output directory for dumps. Default: $T_PGSQL_OUTPUT_DIR, otherwise
<script directory>/../data/dumps.
.TP
.BI \-\-dump\-name " name"
Custom dump file name (without timestamp).
.TP
.BI \-\-keep " n"
Keep the last n local dumps (\-1 keeps all, 0 keeps none; default \-1).
.TP
.BI \-\-archive\-output " directory"
When a dump ages out of
.BR \-\-keep ,
move it to this directory instead of deleting it.
.TP
.BI \-\-archive\-keep " n"
Keep the last n dumps in the archive directory (\-1 keeps all, 0 deletes immediately;
default \-1).
.TP
.BI \-\-from\-keep " n"
For SSH sources: dumps to keep in the remote staging directory (default 1, \-1 keeps
all, 0 deletes).
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-size\-ratio " percent"
Shrink guard.
Fail when the new dump is smaller than
.I percent
of the newest previous dump of the same name in the output directory (integer
0\-100, default 50, 0 disables).
The new dump is kept as
.IR name .suspect
instead of being deleted, no older dump is rotated or removed (keep, retention,
archive and source\-side cleanup are all skipped), a failure notification is sent and
the command exits non\-zero.
Run once with
.B \-\-min\-size\-ratio 0
if the shrink is expected; the next dump then becomes the baseline.
Sizes are compared as stored, so switching
.B \-\-compress
can trigger the guard once.
The same check applies to
.BR clone .
.TP
.BI \-\-from\-stale " time"
With
.BR "\-\-from\-keep 0" ,
purge this job's leftover staging dumps older than
.I time
before dumping (default 72h, 0 disables). Examples: 48h, 2d.
.TP
.BI \-\-skip\-if\-recent " time"
Skip the dump if one exists within the timeframe (24h, 12h, 1d, today).
.TP
.BR \-\-from\-file " [\fIpattern\fR]"
Select an existing dump for the
.B fetch
command. Without a value the latest dump for the database is used. The pattern is a
file name or a glob.
.TP
.BI \-\-file " path"
Dump file to restore, or archive to show with
.BR meta .
.SH RETENTION OPTIONS (GFS)
.TP
.B \-\-retention
Enable Grandfather-Father-Son retention. The newest dump is always kept.
.TP
.BI \-\-retention\-daily " n"
Daily backups to keep (default 7).
.TP
.BI \-\-retention\-weekly " n"
Weekly backups to keep (default 4).
.TP
.BI \-\-retention\-monthly " n"
Monthly backups to keep (default 12).
.TP
.BI \-\-retention\-yearly " n"
Yearly backups to keep (default 3).
.SH HEALTH CHECK OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-health\-check
Check the connection before the operation (default).
.TP
.B \-\-health\-check\-after
Also check after the operation.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-health\-check
Disable health checks.
.TP
.B \-\-health\-check\-fail
Abort when a health check fails.
.SH NOTIFICATION OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-notify " channel"
Notification channel; may be given several times. Channels: telegram,
telegram:TOKEN:CHAT, webhook:URL, email:ADDRESS, slack:URL.
.TP
.B \-\-notify\-on\-error
Notify only on errors.
.TP
.B \-\-notify\-summary
Send one summary notification after a batch.
.SH MASKING OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-mask
Mask sensitive data after the restore. Not supported with
.BR \-\-stream .
.TP
.BI \-\-mask\-rules " file"
JSON file with masking rules.
.TP
.BI \-\-mask\-tables " tables"
Tables to mask (comma-separated).
.SH STREAMING AND TRANSFER OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-stream
Pipe pg_dump into pg_restore without temporary files.
.TP
.BI \-\-stream\-buffer " MB"
Buffer size in megabytes (default 64; needs pv).
.TP
.BI \-\-bwlimit " rate"
Cap transfer bandwidth: 10m (MByte/s), 500k (KByte/s) or a bare number (KByte/s).
Applies to scp copies and, with pv installed, to the streaming pipe.
.TP
.BI \-\-retries " n"
Retry a failed scp transfer n extra times, with backoff (default 0).
.SH MIGRATION OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-globals
Also migrate cluster globals (roles, tablespaces) to every target. Implied by
.BR upgrade .
.TP
.BI \-\-pg\-bindir " directory"
Use the PostgreSQL client tools in this directory for local operations. It does not
affect SSH sources.
.TP
.B \-\-sudo
On SSH sources, run pg_dump through "sudo \-u <db user>" instead of password
authentication (peer authentication).
.SH BATCH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-yaml " name"
Use this jobs file. A bare name resolves to <script directory>/name.yaml; a value
containing a slash or ending in .yaml is used as given. Default: jobs.yaml next to
the script.
.TP
.BI \-\-save " name"
Save the current command line as job
.I name
in the jobs file instead of running it.
.TP
.BR \-\-batch " \fIjob\fR|all"
Run saved job(s); the same as
.B batch
.IR job | all .
.TP
.BI \-\-parallel " n"
Number of jobs to run at the same time (default 1).
.TP
.B \-\-continue\-on\-error
Do not stop the batch when a job fails.
.TP
.BI \-\-only\-jobs " jobs"
Run only these jobs (comma-separated). Alias:
.BR \-\-only .
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude\-jobs " jobs"
Skip these jobs (comma-separated). Alias:
.BR \-\-exclude .
.SH HEARTBEAT PING OPTIONS
Heartbeat pings (a dead-man's switch for a monitor such as healthchecks.io or an
Uptime Kuma push monitor) are sent by
.BR dump ,
.BR clone ,
.BR restore ,
.B upgrade
and
.BR fetch .
The start ping is sent after validation and after the lock was taken; then exactly
one success ping (exit status 0) or failure ping (any other exit status, including
SIGINT and SIGTERM) follows.
Nothing is sent with
.BR \-\-dry\-run ,
for a run skipped by the lock (exit status 75), or by
.BR batch ,
.BR list ,
.BR doctor ,
.B status
and the other commands.
.B \-\-quiet
does not disable pings.
A ping that cannot be delivered only prints a warning and never changes the exit
status.
Each ping is an HTTP POST made by curl with a 10 second limit and two retries, so a
monitor that never answers can delay a run by up to about 30 seconds per ping.
The URL is passed to curl on standard input, never on its command line, and is masked
as scheme://host/*** in logs and in
.BR "jobs show" .
.TP
.BI \-\-ping " url"
Base monitor URL (http:// or https:// only).
The start ping goes to
.IR url /start,
the success ping to
.I url
and the failure ping to
.IR url /fail.
The suffix is inserted before a query string or fragment.
Config file key and jobs file key:
.BR ping .
.TP
.BI \-\-ping\-start " url"
Explicit start URL; wins over the one derived from
.BR \-\-ping .
Key:
.BR ping_start .
.TP
.BI \-\-ping\-fail " url"
Explicit failure URL; wins over the one derived from
.BR \-\-ping .
Key:
.BR ping_fail .
Without
.BR \-\-ping ,
.B \-\-ping\-start
and
.B \-\-ping\-fail
still work on their own: only the start and the failure ping are sent and nothing is
sent on success.
.PP
.B batch
does not accept these options (it exits with 1).
Put
.BR ping: ,
.B ping_start:
and
.B ping_fail:
on the job, or under
.BR defaults: ,
in the jobs file; each job then pings on its own run.
A job value wins over
.BR defaults: .
.SH RUN LOCK OPTIONS
.BR dump ,
.BR fetch ,
.BR restore ,
.B clone
and
.B upgrade
take a lock before working, so two runs never work on the same dump or target at the
same time.
.B dump
and
.B fetch
lock the output directory together with the dump name;
.BR restore ,
.B clone
and
.B upgrade
lock every
.B \-\-to
database (a non-streaming clone or upgrade also locks the dump name it writes).
The lock is not taken with
.BR \-\-dry\-run ,
by
.B batch
itself, or by the other commands.
If the lock is held, the run prints a warning and exits with status 75; no failure
notification and no ping is sent, and the history records the result
.BR locked .
A lock whose owner is gone (process not running, process id reused, or older than
.BR T_PGSQL_LOCK_MAX_AGE )
is taken over by the next run.
If the state directory cannot be used, the run continues without a lock and prints a
warning.
.TP
.BI \-\-lock\-wait " seconds"
Wait up to this many seconds for a held lock before skipping the run (default 0:
skip at once). A non-negative integer.
Config and jobs file key:
.BR lock_wait .
With
.B batch
the option is forwarded to every job that the batch starts.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-lock
Do not take the lock.
Config and jobs file key:
.B no_lock
(true).
With
.B batch
the option is forwarded to every job.
.PP
In a batch, a job that exits with status 75 is reported as skipped (locked), is not
a failure and does not stop the batch.
.SH STATUS OPTIONS
Used by the
.B status
command.
.TP
.BI \-\-yaml " file"
List exactly the jobs of this jobs file; a job that never ran is shown as
.BR never .
Without it, the names come from the history and from the dump files in the output
directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-output " directory"
Dump directory used for the DUMPS KEPT column and the counts of
.B .suspect
and
.B .invalid
files. A missing directory is not an error.
.TP
.BI \-\-name " name"
Show only this name (an unknown name is shown as
.BR never ).
.TP
.BI \-\-limit " n"
Show the last n runs of
.BR \-\-name ,
newest first. Requires
.BR \-\-name ;
cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-max\-age .
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print a JSON array instead of the table.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-age " duration"
Monitoring check: exit with status 3 when a listed name has no fresh successful run,
has never run, or when nothing is listed.
.I duration
is an integer with an optional unit s, m, h or d (upper or lower case); a bare number
is seconds (for example 3600, 90s, 30m, 26h, 1d).
Only runs with result
.B ok
and
.B skipped
(a run skipped by
.BR \-\-skip\-if\-recent )
count as fresh; failed, suspect and locked runs do not.
.PP
.B status
needs bash 4 or newer (on an older bash it prints a message and exits with status 2).
The history is written by every
.BR dump ,
.BR clone ,
.BR restore ,
.B upgrade
and
.B fetch
run (not with
.BR \-\-dry\-run ),
including failed, suspect and lock-skipped runs.
Messages are shortened to 200 bytes and stripped of passwords, URLs and
user@host words.
A run killed by SIGKILL, or lost to power failure, leaves no line.
.SH BOT OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-token " token"
Telegram bot token. Otherwise it is read from the jobs file or from
.BR TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN .
.TP
.BI \-\-cooldown " time"
Minimum interval between button-triggered runs of the same job, as N[h|m|d]
(default 1h).
.SH GENERAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-log " file"
Write log output to a file.
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-level " level"
Log level: debug, info, warn or error.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Verbose output.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Minimal console output. Success notifications are suppressed; failures are still
notified.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Skip confirmations.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Overwrite an existing target database. The restore goes into a temporary database
that replaces the old one only when it succeeded. Without this option an existing
target is an error.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Show what would be done without doing it.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-meta
Do not write metadata.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the help message.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the version.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Back up a local database:
t-pgsql dump \-\-from postgres@localhost/mydb \-\-output ./dumps
.TP
Clone a remote database to a local one:
t-pgsql clone \-\-from 'ssh://admin@192.0.2.20/postgres@localhost/prod' \-\-to postgres@localhost/dev \-\-from\-password\-file ~/.secrets/prod.pass \-\-to\-password\-file ~/.secrets/local.pass \-\-force
.TP
Exclude table data:
t-pgsql clone \-\-from postgres@localhost/prod \-\-to postgres@localhost/dev \-\-exclude\-data "logs,sessions,audit.*" \-\-force
.TP
Migrate to a newer major version:
t-pgsql upgrade \-\-from postgres@old.example.com:5432/appdb \-\-to postgres@new.example.com:5432/appdb \-y
.TP
Run all jobs, two at a time:
t-pgsql batch all \-\-yaml prod \-\-parallel 2
.TP
Check the environment:
t-pgsql doctor
.TP
Learn what a command does:
t-pgsql explain clone
.TP
Heartbeat to a monitor, and skip if another run is active:
t-pgsql dump \-\-from postgres@localhost/mydb \-\-ping https://hc.example.com/ping/UUID \-\-lock\-wait 60
.TP
Show the last run of each job:
t-pgsql status \-\-yaml jobs.yaml
.TP
Cron check that fails when a job has no successful run within 26 hours:
t-pgsql status \-\-yaml jobs.yaml \-\-max\-age 26h \-\-json >/dev/null || echo stale
.TP
Run the Telegram bot:
t-pgsql bot \-\-yaml prod \-\-cooldown 30m
.SH FILES
.TP
.I jobs.yaml
Batch jobs file located next to the t-pgsql script. Override the path with
.BR \-\-yaml .
See
.IR jobs.yaml.example .
.TP
.I Dump files
Written to the output directory as .tar.gz (gzip), or as .dump, .dump.zst, .dump.xz
and .dump.bz2. Dumps that are not wrapped in a .tar.gz have a .meta metadata file
next to them.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/t-pgsql
State directory (default ~/.local/state/t-pgsql; override with
.BR T_PGSQL_STATE_DIR ).
Mode 0700; it is refused when it is a symbolic link or owned by another user.
A run under another HOME (for example through sudo) uses another state directory.
.TP
.I history.tsv
In the state directory. One tab-separated line per run of dump, clone, restore,
upgrade and fetch: epoch, ISO 8601 UTC time, command, name, status (ok, failed,
suspect, skipped, locked), exit code, duration in seconds, size in bytes, message.
Mode 0600. Above 2000 lines it is cut to the newest 1000.
.TP
.I locks/
In the state directory. One
.I NAME-CHECKSUM.lock
directory per held run lock, removed on release. After a SIGKILL it stays and is
taken over by the next run.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command completed successfully. This also applies to
.BR \-\-help ,
.B \-\-version
and
.B explain
with a known topic, and to
.B doctor
when it found at most warnings.
.TP
.B 1
An option or value was invalid, a required value or argument was missing, the command
was unknown, or the operation failed.
.B batch
exits with 1 when at least one job failed, also with
.BR \-\-continue\-on\-error .
.B doctor
exits with 1 when it found a problem.
.PP
Other non-zero values may be returned when an underlying tool such as pg_restore or
ssh fails; do not rely on a specific value other than 0, 2, 3 and 75.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error of
.BR status :
bad
.B \-\-max\-age
or
.BR \-\-limit ,
.B \-\-limit
without
.BR \-\-name ,
a missing
.B \-\-yaml
file, an unknown option or stray argument, or a bash older than 4.
.TP
.B 3
.B status \-\-max\-age
found a listed name without a fresh successful run (or nothing listed).
.TP
.B 75
The run was skipped because another run holds the lock. This is not a failure
(EX_TEMPFAIL): a cron wrapper should treat it as success or retry later.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B T_PGSQL_PASSWORD
Password for both source and target (overridden by the \-\-password options).
.TP
.B T_PGSQL_FROM_PASSWORD
Source password (overridden by \-\-from\-password).
.TP
.B T_PGSQL_TO_PASSWORD
Target password (overridden by \-\-to\-password).
.TP
.B T_PGSQL_OUTPUT_DIR
Default output directory for dumps (overridden by \-\-output).
.TP
.B T_PGSQL_STATE_DIR
State directory for the run history and the run locks (default
$XDG_STATE_HOME/t-pgsql, or ~/.local/state/t-pgsql).
.TP
.B T_PGSQL_LOCK_MAX_AGE
Maximum age in seconds after which a run lock counts as stale and is taken over
(default 86400).
.TP
.B T_PGSQL_PING_DISABLE
Set to 1 to disable all heartbeat pings.
.TP
.B PGCONNECT_TIMEOUT
libpq connection timeout in seconds (default 10).
.TP
.B TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN
Bot token for the bare
.B \-\-notify telegram
channel and for
.BR bot .
.TP
.B TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID
Chat id for the bare
.B \-\-notify telegram
channel.
.TP
.B TELEGRAM_THREAD_ID
Optional forum-topic thread id for Telegram notifications.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Directory for temporary files (default /tmp).
.TP
.B HOME
Used to expand a leading ~ in path values read from configuration files.
